1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Welcome to Module 10. A trained model is not a product. The jump from a notebook that hits 94 percent on a held-out set to a service that answers a thousand requests per second with predictable latency, version history, and rollback safety is where most machine learning projects actually die. This module covers the deployment stack end to end: packaging, optimization, serving, pipelines, and monitoring.

2
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Start with the artifact. TensorFlow's SavedModel format captures the graph, the weights, the optimizer state, and — most importantly — named signatures. A signature is a typed entry point: an input spec, an output spec, and a callable. You can export multiple signatures from the same model — serve for realtime inference, score_batch for offline batch, get_embedding for retrieval. Signatures make the serving surface explicit and verifiable; without them you are shipping a pickle.

3
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:46.000
TensorFlow Serving is the reference production server. It loads SavedModels, exposes them over gRPC and REST, handles versioning automatically by watching a versioned directory structure, and supports batched inference for throughput. Batching parameters — max_batch_size, batch_timeout_micros, num_batch_threads — are the three knobs that trade latency for throughput. Tune them against your actual traffic, not against synthetic load; production traffic is bursty in ways benchmarks rarely capture.

4
00:00:46.000 --> 00:01:02.000
For edge and mobile, the story changes. TensorFlow Lite converts a SavedModel to a FlatBuffer optimized for on-device inference. The runtime is small — around one megabyte — supports hardware delegates for GPU, NNAPI on Android, Core ML on iOS, and Edge TPU, and has a microcontroller variant that runs on a few kilobytes of RAM. The conversion is lossy in subtle ways: unsupported ops force fallback to CPU, and custom ops require explicit registration.

5
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Quantization is the single highest-leverage optimization. Post-training dynamic-range quantization halves the model size and gives modest speedups on CPU. Post-training float16 quantization is the GPU-friendly version with near-zero accuracy loss. Full-integer int8 quantization is where the serious gains live — four times smaller, two to four times faster, and the only option for most microcontrollers — but it requires a representative dataset to calibrate activation ranges.

6
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:34.000
When post-training int8 drops accuracy more than you can absorb, move to quantization-aware training. QAT inserts fake-quant nodes during training so the model learns to be robust to the quantization it will see at inference. The final export is then a true int8 graph. Expect a small training-time overhead and a handful of tuning passes, but for classification and detection models the accuracy recovery versus post-training quant is typically one to three points — often the difference between shippable and not.

7
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Pruning removes weights. Magnitude pruning masks the smallest weights to zero during training; structured pruning removes entire filters or attention heads so the speedup survives dense-kernel execution. Weight clustering maps all weights to a small codebook and stores the codebook plus an index per weight. Both techniques compose with quantization — prune first, then quantize — and together can deliver ten-times compression with single-digit accuracy loss on well-behaved models.

8
00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Graph optimization is the free lunch. Grappler rewrites the TensorFlow graph at load time: constant folding, common subexpression elimination, fusion of Conv-BN-Relu into a single op, layout optimization, and shape inference. XLA goes further by compiling subgraphs to optimized kernels for CPU, GPU, and TPU. For Transformer-heavy workloads, enabling jit_compile=True on the serving path can yield substantial speedups with no code changes beyond a decorator.

9
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:22.000
A model in production is half of the system; the pipeline that produces it is the other half. TFX — TensorFlow Extended — is the reference pipeline framework. Its components are canonical: ExampleGen for data, StatisticsGen and SchemaGen for validation, Transform for deterministic preprocessing, Trainer, Tuner, Evaluator, and Pusher. ML Metadata — MLMD — records every artifact and execution, so you can trace any production prediction back to the exact data and code that produced the model.

10
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Preprocessing is the most common production bug source. If you transform features one way in training and another way in serving — even by a rounding error — predictions drift silently. The TFX answer is TFTransform, which builds preprocessing into a TensorFlow graph that gets bundled with the model. Training consumes the graph via tf.data, serving consumes the exact same graph, and the two can never diverge. This is called training-serving skew and it is the single biggest source of real-world model failures.

11
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:54.000
Once a model is live you need monitoring. Four signals belong on every dashboard: prediction latency percentiles — P50, P95, P99 — throughput in queries per second, error rate broken out by status code, and prediction distribution statistics by feature. The fourth is the subtle one: a model with ninety-nine-nine percent uptime and drifting feature distributions is silently failing in a way that standard SRE dashboards will miss. Tools like TensorFlow Data Validation run this comparison automatically against the training-time schema.

12
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:10.000
Drift has two flavors. Data drift is a change in the input distribution — a new device, a new season, a new customer segment. Concept drift is a change in the relationship between inputs and the target — last year's purchase patterns no longer predict next year's. Data drift can be detected from inputs alone; concept drift needs labels and therefore a feedback loop. Design the pipeline so labels flow back from production within a window that lets you detect regressions before they become outages.

13
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:26.000
Rollout strategies protect you from bad deploys. Canary rollouts route a small percentage of traffic — one or five percent — to the new model and compare metrics against the incumbent. Shadow deployments send all traffic to both and compare predictions offline without serving the new model's output to users. A/B tests split traffic by user cohort and measure downstream business outcomes, not just model metrics. Pick the pattern that matches your tolerance for user-visible regression.

14
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Versioning is non-negotiable. Every deployed model needs a version pinned to a git commit, a dataset snapshot, and a set of hyperparameters. The deployment system should allow rollback to any previous version with one command. Model registries — MLflow, Vertex AI Model Registry, SageMaker Model Registry, or the open TFX MLMD store — give you this with approval workflows, stage tags like Staging and Production, and audit trails. Treat your model registry like you treat git.

15
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:58.000
Edge deployment adds constraints most cloud engineers never see. Battery budget caps inference frequency; thermal throttling degrades latency under sustained load; unreliable networks force on-device inference with asynchronous sync; privacy regulations often mandate it. The deployment patterns are specific: ship the model inside the app bundle or pull it from a signed CDN, version it independently of the app binary, and instrument enough on-device metrics to detect field regressions without leaking user data.

16
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:14.000
Common pitfalls in this module's labs. First, skipping the representative dataset on int8 quantization — calibration with sixteen random images gives garbage activation ranges. Second, benchmarking latency without warm-up — the first few predictions include graph-build and kernel-compile overhead and are not representative. Third, ignoring tail latency — P50 looks fine while P99 is timing out one user in a hundred. Fourth, assuming your validation accuracy survives quantization — always re-evaluate the post-quant model on the validation set.

17
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:28.000
That closes the course. You built a mental model of deep learning, trained CNNs for vision and RNNs and Transformers for language, generated images with GANs, forecasted sequences, and now deployed all of it. The arc from a one-line Keras Sequential to a monitored TFX pipeline is long, but every link in the chain is now in your hands. Go build something that survives contact with real traffic. That is the entire point.
